  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatApp_4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "After your clinical trial has ended, your doctor explains that the new
    treatment you received has now been approved for wider use. You learn that even
    though approval has been granted, researchers and doctors still carefully track
    how the treatment performs in real-world patients like you.\r\n\r\nWhy do researchers
    continue to monitor treatments after they\u2019ve been approved?\r"
  explanation: Even after approval, ongoing safety monitoring (called post-market
    surveillance) helps ensure new treatments remain safe and effective in the real
    world, especially once they are used by larger, more diverse groups. This allows
    researchers, doctors, and regulatory agencies to track these outcomes closely
    and monitor any new side effects or long-term effects that may appear, helping
    to protect patients and improve how treatments are used over time.
  correctAnswer: To watch for rare or long-term side effects that may not have shown
    up during the trial
  incorrectAnswer1: To decide which patients should be the first to receive the newly
    approved treatment
  incorrectAnswer2: To determine the appropriate cost of the newly approved treatment
  incorrectAnswer3: To test whether patient participants followed the rules of the
    trial
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Approval
